256
00:35:52.860 --> 00:36:17.790
Richard Bair: And in here, there's a whole section on foundational concepts, events, transactions, finality, liveness under stress, what happens when we're under CPU pressure, execution is slow, all this kind of stuff. And in this document, we break out all the different modules, the core modules that make up the consensus layer. Not all of these are implemented yet in code.

257
00:36:17.790 --> 00:36:31.099
Richard Bair: This is just the design of, you know, what we're building. So, for example, the sheriff component doesn't actually exist yet, but it's getting built, whereas these other four components already exist today and are operating.

258
00:36:31.610 --> 00:36:38.970
Richard Bair: Now, the, … if we go look down in the… let's go find it, stale…

259
00:36:40.290 --> 00:36:58.920
Richard Bair: there's a definition in here of what a stale event is, and how the stale events work. So I'm just going to describe it, I'm not going to read it to you, but just so… I want to make sure everybody was aware of this document. This is a really good thing to read and understand if you're interested in, sort of, the guts of, sort of, you know, the foundational pieces of how Consensus Layer is built.

260
00:36:59.460 --> 00:37:05.539
Richard Bair: … When you create… if you are a node.

261
00:37:05.770 --> 00:37:10.950
Richard Bair: you receive transactions from the outside world. Okay, here we go.

262
00:37:11.170 --> 00:37:21.030
Richard Bair: And when you receive transactions from the outside world, you bundle those transactions inside a thing called an event, and you gossip the event to all the other nodes in the network.

263
00:37:21.730 --> 00:37:36.779
Richard Bair: And, gossip, of course, could be, like, complete broadcast, where you send it to every single node, which is what we do today, but there's a point where your network gets so large that it makes more sense for you to have several hops. That's all sort of implementation detail in Gossip.

264
00:37:36.780 --> 00:37:44.379
Richard Bair: The key thing is, is that you take some set of transactions, you put it in an event, and you gossip that event to everybody. And…

265
00:37:44.710 --> 00:37:47.529
Richard Bair: Well, you receive events from other people.

266
00:37:47.760 --> 00:38:05.230
Richard Bair: And, events have, parents, you know, there's a self-parent and another parent, and this is what forms the hash graph. So when we see the hashgraph A2, you know, all these A events are created by Alice, and all these are created by Bob, the B events by Bob, and so forth.

267
00:38:05.230 --> 00:38:23.120
Richard Bair: And when I create a new event, let's say I'm creating a 2 event, I'm Alice, I'm going to have one parent is going to be one of my own events, and another parent is going to be somebody else's event. So they're always building on each other's events, that's how you get gossip about gossip, and the whole hashgraph algorithm works on this principle.

268
00:38:23.120 --> 00:38:32.209
Richard Bair: But it's important to keep in mind that we're not gossiping transactions individually, we're bundling them inside of events and gossiping events.

269
00:38:32.210 --> 00:38:39.009
Richard Bair: Okay. Now, what happens if I have an event, and it has a whole pile of transactions in it, and I gossip it to the world?

270
00:38:39.180 --> 00:38:44.489
Richard Bair: And nobody builds on my event. And by that, I mean nobody has an event.

271
00:38:44.890 --> 00:38:47.970
Richard Bair: That uses me as the other parent.

272
00:38:48.960 --> 00:38:53.200
Richard Bair: Well, let's take a look at B1 right here. See B1?

273
00:38:53.570 --> 00:39:09.389
Richard Bair: he… nobody's using him as another parent. B2 has it as a self-parent, but nobody has it as another parent, right? Not C2, not D2, not A2, none of… they've all dodged… or D1. Nobody built on D1 except for D2 itself.

274
00:39:09.440 --> 00:39:22.200
Richard Bair: So at this moment in time, from the perspective of these events, B1 and D1, nobody else in the network has seen it, so those transactions cannot come to consensus.

275
00:39:22.730 --> 00:39:31.699
Richard Bair: But we build another layer of, you know, there's another round of gossip, we create more events, they have other self-parents, and now you can see that C3…

276
00:39:31.830 --> 00:39:46.639
Richard Bair: can see D1 now. Okay, so D1 is seen by D2, and it's seen by C3, but it's still not seen by anybody else at this point in time, so not enough people have seen D1.

277
00:39:46.740 --> 00:40:04.159
Richard Bair: But if we go up another layer, what has happened? Well, B4 sees D3, sees D2, sees D1. Okay, now I have two-thirds of the network who's seen it. B4's seen it, C3's seen it, D2's seen it, greater than two-thirds. So that means that D1 is now good, and those transactions could be involved.

278
00:40:04.160 --> 00:40:19.479
Richard Bair: But what happens if we went many rounds and not enough people see this event? They just haven't chosen it by virtue of whatever events they built their events on top of. We just never got to the point where this guy was seen.

279
00:40:20.470 --> 00:40:25.720
Richard Bair: If that happens, then the transactions inside the… the event itself becomes stale.

280
00:40:26.290 --> 00:40:43.160
Richard Bair: And because we don't want to have, sort of, an infinite number of, you know, old events that we hold onto, just hoping somebody eventually builds on it, we have a limit, and it's configurable on the network. I think we have it at 26.

281
00:40:43.160 --> 00:40:54.790
Richard Bair: Which is called the Ancient Round. So basically, if you've gone 26 rounds, and you have an event that hasn't had sufficient visibility from the rest of the network, then the transactions inside of it become stale.

282
00:40:55.280 --> 00:40:59.069
Richard Bair: On mainnet, I'm not sure if this has ever happened.

283
00:40:59.440 --> 00:41:09.079
Richard Bair: In performance testing environments, it happens. We're generating events at such a rapid pace that sometimes it happens.

284
00:41:09.180 --> 00:41:25.849
Richard Bair: And, we have metric to keep track of that, but, it creates some problems for the performance test client. And also, since it is theoretically possible to happen, it's important to make sure that developers have a way of dealing with still events.

285
00:41:25.850 --> 00:41:38.639
Richard Bair: Today, you don't have any visibility into this at all. As an application developer, you submit a transaction to the network, you go ask for its receipt. When you get that receipt back.

286
00:41:39.220 --> 00:41:44.109
Richard Bair: It either says unknown, which means, it's…

287
00:41:44.380 --> 00:42:08.360
Richard Bair: it's really a horrible name for the thing, but anyway, it's a very old name, we can't change it now. But unknown doesn't really mean it's unknown, it means the status is unknown. We actually have… basically, if you query a node and it gives you back a transaction receipt that says unknown, that means, yes, I have this transaction, no, I don't yet know what its state is. Did it succeed? Did it fail?

288
00:42:08.360 --> 00:42:16.920
Richard Bair: And the reason I don't know that is because it hasn't come to consensus yet. So instead of unknown, we should have said awaiting Consensus or something, that would have been a better name for it.

289
00:42:16.920 --> 00:42:17.690
Richard Bair: …

290
00:42:17.690 --> 00:42:33.610
Richard Bair: But, anyway, that's what unknown is. So, and then once… once it has come to consensus, then we know what its disposition is. Did it succeed? You know, did it fail? If it failed, which of 500 error codes was it? You know, there's… there's information about that.

291
00:42:34.340 --> 00:42:38.769
Richard Bair: But we don't ever talk about stale. Stale events never come to consensus.

